WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:06.990
Let's take a look at how to structure your file system.

2
00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:08.610
You should organize your user files

3
00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.690
into multiple HFS datasets.

4
00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:13.150
This better enables you to manage space,

5
00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:16.890
control contention, and manage backup and recovery situations.

6
00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.410
That said, this is just one way of managing an environment,

7
00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.130
not the only one, and depending on individual requirements

8
00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:25.050
might not necessarily be the best way.

9
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.450
For example, once the US dataset per user

10
00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.050
might be difficult to manage,

11
00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:31.290
another approach might be to divide the data

12
00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:33.610
by application or by department.

13
00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:36.550
Regardless, in many cases, this is the way to go.

14
00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.170
The slash U directory in the root file system

15
00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:42.610
should be an empty directory mount point for an HFS

16
00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:44.730
that contains just directories or mount points

17
00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.210
for each of your users.

18
00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:49.810
This HFS dataset will be mounted automatically

19
00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:52.110
at OMVS initialization.

20
00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.730
As you add users to your system,

21
00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.850
create a new HFS for each one,

22
00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:58.810
mount that HFS at the specific mount point set aside

23
00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:01.170
for that user, and then change the ownership

24
00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:04.490
of the mount point directory to the user's ID.

25
00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:06.210
This can be done in the Rex Exec

26
00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.250
to simplify the process of adding a new user.

27
00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.330
The advantages of this organization are that user files

28
00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:15.650
are stored in a separate ZOS dataset for each user,

29
00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:19.610
and the root dataset can be kept read-only.

30
00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:23.770
Auto-mount is an optional facility that comes with ZOS Unix.

31
00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:26.050
It can be implemented if you want it.

32
00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:30.590
It mounts user datasets and other datasets as directed.

33
00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:33.650
Auto-mount only mounts datasets as they are referenced,

34
00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:36.750
and these mounts are transparent to the user.

35
00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:40.750
Both HFS and ZFS can be auto-mounted.

36
00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:43.190
You can use a single auto-mount policy

37
00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:48.280
to manage both HFS and ZFS file systems.

38
00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.620
The auto-mount facility is a way to ensure

39
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:53.520
that a user's file system is mounted automatically

40
00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.020
whenever they access a file in it.

41
00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:00.100
The support group builds a file to identify the users,

42
00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.980
their file systems, and its mount characteristics.

43
00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:05.980
When the auto-mount facility is started,

44
00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.480
it monitors file access.

45
00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.460
When a user attempts to access one of their files,

46
00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.980
auto-mount recognizes the need for a mount.

47
00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.800
If there's no activity to a file system

48
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.260
for a divine period of time,

49
00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:22.920
well, then auto-mount can also unmount the file system.

50
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.140
This avoids the need for a super user

51
00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:28.240
to mount file systems upon request.

52
00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.320
Let's look at another facility

53
00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.320
for enhanced performance of programs that access files.

54
00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:39.340
Memory mapped files enable multiple processes

55
00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.960
to share file data without each doing its own reads

56
00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:44.440
and writes.

57
00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:48.720
This is a function from the XPG 4.2 standard.

58
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.040
The kernel stores the pages of data

59
00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.960
from the file in a data space.

60
00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.760
A child process inherits the same mapping

61
00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.920
as defined by the parent using the mmmap function.

62
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.640
Another process will need to open the file to map

63
00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.000
to the same data pages in the data space.

64
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.280
There are associated commands to ensure

65
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.720
that the physical data on the storage device

66
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.140
is updated as necessary.

67
00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.360
Now, this diagram provides a logical view

68
00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.320
of the file system structure

69
00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.200
and an overview of its interfaces.

70
00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:27.000
A ZOS Unix user has multiple interfaces

71
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.480
to the hierarchical file system.

72
00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.360
The POSIX interfaces are the C program functions

73
00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.080
and the shell commands.

74
00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.400
And there's the ZOS interfaces through the TSCoE commands

75
00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:38.840
and from high level languages,

76
00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.240
which can use ZOS callable services

77
00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:44.220
to access the hierarchical files.

78
00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.320
The hierarchical file system is divided

79
00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.280
into a logical file system as LFS

80
00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:52.880
and a physical file system PFS.

81
00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.120
LFS presents a single interface to the file system.

82
00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.520
LFS receives the access request from a user

83
00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.120
and maps the request to the appropriate PFS.

84
00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:04.960
The LFS assigns a descriptor to the request

85
00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.400
from the file descriptor table.

86
00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:10.360
PFS performs the actual work in the file system.

87
00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:13.880
There are multiple PFSs in the file system.

88
00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:16.360
HFS files support files on DASD.

89
00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:19.360
PIPES support interprocess communication.

90
00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.160
Character special files support terminal communication

91
00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.920
and socket file systems support program

92
00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:26.600
to program communications.

93
00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:30.760
Next up, we're talking backup and recovery.
